            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Portuguese for to rebut, then the Portuguese for relevant."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: o efeito",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "o efeito — \"the effect\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "O remédio fez efeito depressa. The medicine took effect quickly."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Fazer efeito — to take effect; com efeito — indeed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "efeito is inherited from Latin effectus \"an effect, a result\" (EtymDB), from efficere \"to bring about\", built from ex and facere \"to do, to make\" (Lewis & Short); -ct- became -it- as in feito."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
